---
import LegalServiceLayout from '../../components/LegalServiceLayout.astro';
---
<LegalServiceLayout title="Wisconsin Custody & Placement Attorney" description="Understand Wisconsin legal custody, physical placement, shared schedules, and GAL insight with virtual legal counsel from Patrick Rowe." canonicalPath="/legal-services/custody-placement" breadcrumbLabel="Custody & Placement" headline="Protecting What Matters Most: Wisconsin Custody & Placement" subheadline="Clear, compassionate, and strategic legal advocacy for your parental rights—delivered entirely through a secure virtual law practice." ctaHeading="Find a practical path forward for your family.">
  <section class="container-shell py-16 sm:py-24" aria-labelledby="difference-title">
    <h2 id="difference-title" class="max-w-4xl text-3xl leading-tight text-forest sm:text-4xl">Understanding the Difference: Custody vs. Placement</h2>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="border border-forest/20 bg-white p-7 sm:p-9">
        <h3 class="text-3xl leading-tight text-forest">Legal Custody (Decision-Making)</h3><p class="mt-6 text-base leading-8 text-onyx/80">Legal custody concerns authority over major life decisions, including healthcare, education, and religion. It is separate from the child’s day-to-day placement schedule.</p>
        <dl class="mt-8 space-y-7 border-t border-forest/15 pt-7"><div><dt class="font-semibold text-forest">Joint Legal Custody</dt><dd class="mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-onyx/80">Parents share authority and must consult and agree on major decisions, subject to any specific allocation of authority in the court order.</dd></div><div><dt class="font-semibold text-forest">Sole Legal Custody</dt><dd class="mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-onyx/80">One parent has independent authority to make major decisions, within the terms of the court order.</dd></div></dl>
      </article>
      <article class="border border-gold/50 bg-forest p-7 text-parchment sm:p-9">
        <h3 class="text-3xl leading-tight">Physical Placement (Where the Child Lives)</h3><p class="mt-6 text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">Physical placement is the actual schedule of time the child spends in each parent’s care, including ordinary routines, holidays, and school breaks.</p>
        <dl class="mt-8 space-y-7 border-t border-gold/30 pt-7"><div><dt class="font-semibold text-gold-light">Shared Placement</dt><dd class="mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">For shared-placement child support calculations, each parent generally has court-ordered placement of at least 25% of the year—92 or more days, measured through overnights or qualifying equivalent care—and shares basic support costs.</dd></div><div><dt class="font-semibold text-gold-light">Primary Placement</dt><dd class="mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">The child lives with one parent most of the time. A schedule giving that parent more than 75% of the year ordinarily leaves the other parent below the shared-placement threshold; “primary placement” is not universally defined by a strict 75% cutoff.</dd></div></dl>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container-shell pb-4" aria-labelledby="gal-insight-title"><div class="border-l-4 border-gold bg-forest-dark p-7 text-parchment sm:p-12"><h2 id="gal-insight-title" class="text-3xl leading-tight sm:text-4xl">The Rowe Law Advantage (GAL Insight)</h2><p class="mt-7 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-9 text-parchment/85">Patrick Rowe is frequently appointed by Wisconsin courts as a Guardian ad Litem. This work brings deep insight into how GALs investigate a family’s circumstances and formulate best-interest recommendations for judges.</p><p class="mt-5 max-w-4xl text-base leading-8 text-parchment/80">That perspective informs a focused, child-first approach to preparing your case. When representing a parent, Patrick acts as that parent’s advocate; a GAL appointment is a separate court-directed role.</p></div></section>
</LegalServiceLayout>
